# Optional persistence capabilities

The selected adapters are recorded in `app/infrastructure/persistence_settings.py`
and the project's `project.yaml`. Only selected capabilities add dependencies and
services. Application runtime never imports the harness.

`app.state.resources` owns the repository, vector store and cache selected at
generation time. Inject these ports into your use cases; do not import adapters
from the domain/application layers. `create_app(resources=...)` supports test
injection. Engine pools and Redis clients close on application shutdown.
There are no public record CRUD endpoints or implicit business entities.

## Repository

`Repository` supports atomic `put(Record(key, value))`, `get(key)` and idempotent
`delete(key)`. Values are strings; missing keys return None. Keys have 1–200
characters, are case-sensitive, and cannot contain NUL. Values cannot contain
NUL either. Multi-operation transactions, pagination and multi-tenancy are not
part of this small reference contract. Namespaces isolate records; they are not
an authorization mechanism.

- Memory: a process-local reference adapter, no durability or shared workers.
- SQLite: SQLAlchemy with a file on the `sqlite-data` volume; bounded lock wait.
- PostgreSQL: SQLAlchemy and psycopg, bounded connections and statement timeouts.

Both SQL dialects use bound parameters and explicit per-operation transactions.
The same repository contracts run against memory, SQLite and, when selected,
PostgreSQL. Unit tests create temporary SQLite databases; integration tests use
unique namespaces and delete only their own rows. Never point integration tests
at production.

## Migrations and local startup

For SQLite/PostgreSQL:

```sh
# Run from the generated project root. Starts required DB/cache containers.
make migrate
make up
make test
make test-contracts
# For PostgreSQL and/or Redis, exercises the real Compose services:
make test-integration
make down
```

Memory/cache-only projects have no migrate target. SQL migrations are explicit;
normal API startup never creates tables. Until migration, `/api/health` stays
live but `/api/readiness` reports unavailable SQL storage. `make migrate` runs
Alembic upgrade-to-head and serializes PostgreSQL migration runners with an
advisory transaction lock. It preserves existing records when repeated.
`make down` preserves database volumes. Deleting a volume or running Alembic
downgrade can destroy data; neither is part of the normal lifecycle.

To create a new revision inside the API's Python environment:

```sh
python -m alembic revision -m "describe schema change"
# Edit the generated revision, review it, then:
python -m app.infrastructure.migrate
```

`DATABASE_URL` is read from the environment, never `project.yaml`. Without Docker,
create the local `data/` directory for SQLite and set
`DATABASE_URL=sqlite:///data/app.db` before migrating. PostgreSQL URLs use the
`postgresql+psycopg://` scheme. The Dockerfile includes migrations in runtime.

Compose database credentials are explicitly disposable local defaults, not a
production credential strategy. DB/cache have no published host ports. No cloud
database or Redis deployment is configured. SQL data survives `make down`/`up`;
Redis is disposable cache with persistence disabled.

## pgvector

When selected, revision 0002 enables the vector extension and a separate table.
The initial vector contract uses **three dimensions**, Euclidean distance and
exact search; it is a minimal executable example, not an embedding model default.
Change the dimension constant, migration and contracts together before using a
real embedding model. Components must be finite in [-1e10, 1e10]; result limits
are integers 1–100. Ties are ordered by key. Float32 storage means distance
comparisons should use a tolerance. No approximate index is created.

`VectorStore` is separate from the record repository. Shared search/upsert/delete
contracts run against the in-memory reference and pgvector. SQLite migrations
skip the PostgreSQL-only vector table. Downgrade removes the vector table but
keeps the extension, which other applications may share.

## Redis cache

`Cache` stores opaque bytes with a mandatory integer TTL of 1–86400 seconds;
overwriting resets the TTL. Missing/expired keys return None. Namespace prefixes
are encoded to avoid collisions. The adapter has bounded socket timeouts and
no retries; failures are visible to callers and readiness reports unavailable.
Tests compare Redis with a memory fake, including expiration and binary values.
Test keys expire automatically; tests never use FLUSHDB.

`make test` runs local tests, reporting unavailable external contracts as skipped.
`make test-integration` supplies TEST_DATABASE_URL/TEST_REDIS_URL for the selected
Compose services so their contracts actually run. Migrate SQL first. Manually
running pytest against external services requires those explicit test variables.
